{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices (often by ~2–10 EUR), especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/mean-reversion mismatch: in sustained sell-offs the model underestimates downside (too slow to follow the downtrend) and keeps reverting upward. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 10–20 days show a clear negative slope; allow lower terminal levels.",
    "Lag after regime breaks: when history transitions from ~80s to low-70s/high-60s, the model adapts too slowly and keeps projecting mid-70s. Add a regime-change detector (e.g., large 5–10d drawdown) that accelerates downward adjustment for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: after small upticks in history, the model extrapolates a stronger rebound than realized. Cap rebound magnitude (or dampen positive drift) when medium-term trend remains negative.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-weighted bias correction (minimal at 1–5d, moderate at 6–15d, strongest at 16–30d)."
  ]
}
